<details><summary>What can the tool do? What must we still do? / 工具與人的責任</summary><div class="two"><article><h3>Informing</h3><p>GenAI can offer information and suggestions. Teachers make the final judgement using learner evidence and the language aims/criteria.</p></article><article><h3>Forming</h3><p>GenAI can propose improvement options. Teachers and learners enact purposeful change and check its effect; suggestions alone are not development.</p></article></div><p>Open a practice to compare responsibilities. These are possible forms of assistance, not promises about every tool.</p>{tirfAiRoles.map(([practice,tool,human])=><details><summary>{practice}</summary><div class="two"><article><h3>The tool may help</h3><p>{tool}</p></article><article><h3>We remain responsible</h3><p>{human}</p></article></div></details>)}</details>
<details><summary>Digital tools and GenAI are different affordances</summary><p>Quizzes, clickers, exit tickets, analytics and e-portfolios can support digital assessment. They are not necessarily AI. GenAI can generate new content or code in response to prompts. Neither technology category establishes formative use.</p><p>Labels such as AI-assisted, computer-based, digital, automated, mobile and technology-enhanced assessment describe different tools and arrangements. Ask what evidence is elicited, how it is interpreted, and what purposeful action is enacted.</p></details>
